The use of unofficial retired numbers drew controversy during  [LINK: (Steve Spurrier) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Spurrier] 's first year as head coach in 2002. [LINK: ([222]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-retirednumbers-223]  Quarterbacks  [LINK: (Danny Wuerffel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danny_Wuerffel]  and  [LINK: (Shane Matthews) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shane_Matthews]  first wore 7 and 9 respectively during training camp. The resulting controversy led to them switching to 17 and 6. [LINK: ([222]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-retirednumbers-223]  Quarterback  [LINK: (Dwayne Haskins) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dwayne_Haskins] , the team's first-round draft selection in 2019, wore number 7 with permission from Theismann for his two-year tenure with the team. [LINK: ([223]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-224]

Names in bold indicate induction primarily based on accomplishments with Washington.

When the team left  [LINK: (RFK Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RFK_Stadium]  in 1996, they left behind the signs commemorating the Washington Hall of Stars. The team began a new tradition of honoring Redskins greats via the "Ring of Fame", a set of signs on the upper-level facade at Northwest Stadium. Team founder  [LINK: (George Preston Marshall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Preston_Marshall]  was removed in 2020, the only member to lose his place in the ring. [LINK: ([224]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-225]  [LINK: ([225]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-226]

1981-1993

For the Redskins' 70th anniversary in 2002, a panel selected the "70 Greatest Redskins", player and coaches who were honored in a weekend of festivities, including a halftime ceremony during a Redskins' game against the  [LINK: (Indianapolis Colts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2002_Indianapolis_Colts_season] . [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-greatest-228]  [LINK: ([228]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-229]  In 2012, ten players and personnel were added to the list for the team's 80th anniversary. [LINK: ([229]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-230]  In 2022, ten more were added for the franchise's 90th anniversary. [LINK: ([230]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-231]

The panel that chose the 70 consisted of former news anchor  [LINK: (Bernard Shaw) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_Shaw_(journalist)] ; former player  [LINK: (Bobby Mitchell) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bobby_Mitchell] ; Senator  [LINK: (George Allen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Allen_(U.S._politician)]  (son of coach  [LINK: (George Allen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Allen_(coach)] ); broadcaster  [LINK: (Ken Beatrice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Beatrice] ; Noel Epstein, editor for the  [LINK: (Washington Post) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Post] ; former diplomat  [LINK: (Joseph J. Sisco) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_J._Sisco] ; Phil Hochberg, who retired in 2001 after 38 years as team stadium announcer; Pro Football Hall of Fame historian Joe Horrigan; sportscaster  [LINK: (George Michael) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Michael_(sportscaster)] ; sports director  [LINK: (Andy Pollin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andy_Pollin] ; NFL Films president  [LINK: (Steven Sabol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steven_Sabol] ; and news anchor  [LINK: (Jim Vance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Vance] . [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-greatest-228]

The list includes three head coaches and 67 players, of which 41 were offensive players, 23 defensive players, and three special teams players. [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-greatest-228]  Among the 70 Greatest, there are 92  [LINK: (Super Bowl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Bowl]  appearances, with 47 going once and 45 playing in more than one. 29 members possess one Super Bowl ring and 26 have more than one. Also, before the Super Bowl, members of the 70 made 18 World Championship appearances including six that participated in the Redskins' NFL Championship victories in 1937 and 1942. [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-greatest-228]  Bold indicates people elected to the Pro Football Hall of Fame.


