Offense

The Redskins scored 541 points in 1983, which is the sixth highest total in a season of all time. [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-recordscoring-264]

The Redskins' 72 points against the  [LINK: (New York Giants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Giants]  on November 27, 1966, are the most points ever scored by an NFL team in a regular-season game, and the 72–41 score amounted to 113 points and the highest-scoring game ever in NFL history. [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-recordscoring-264]  The second-half scoring for the game amounted to 65 points, the second-highest point total for second-half scoring and the third-highest total scoring in any half in NFL history. [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-recordscoring-264]  The Redskins' 10 touchdowns are the most by a team in a single game, and the 16 total touchdowns are the most combined for a game. [LINK: ([264]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-265]  The Redskins' nine  [LINK: (PATs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extra_point]  are the second-most all-time for a single game, and the 14 combined PATs are the most ever in a game. [LINK: ([265]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-266]

The Redskins set a record for most first downs in a game with 39 in a game against the Lions on November 4, 1990. They also set a record by not allowing a single first down against the Giants on September 27, 1942. [LINK: ([266]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-267]

The Redskins have led the league in passing eight times: in 1938, 1940, 1944, 1947–48, 1967, 1974 and 1989. Only the  [LINK: (San Diego Chargers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Diego_Chargers]  have led more times. [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-passing-268]  The Redskins led the league in completion percentage 11 times: in 1937, 1939–1940, 1942–45, 1947–48 and 1969–1970, second only to the  [LINK: (San Francisco 49ers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Francisco_49ers] . [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-passing-268]  Their four straight years from 1942 to 1945 is the second longest streak.

The Redskins' nine sacks allowed in 1991 are the third-fewest allowed in a season. [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-passing-268]

The Redskins completed 43 passes in an overtime win against  [LINK: (Detroit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Detroit_Lions]  on November 4, 1990, second-most all-time. [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-passing-268]

Defense

The Redskins recovered eight opponent's fumbles against the  [LINK: (St. Louis Cardinals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Louis_Cardinals_(NFL)]  on October 25, 1976, the most ever in one game. [LINK: ([268]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-269]

The Redskins allowed 82 first downs in  [LINK: (1937) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1937_Washington_Redskins_season] , third fewest all-time. [LINK: ([269]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-270]

The Redskins have led the league in fewest total yards allowed five times, 1935–37, 1939, and 1946, which is the third most. [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-fewestyards-271]  Their three consecutive years from 1935 to 1937 is an NFL record. [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-fewestyards-271]

The Redskins have led the league in fewest passing yards allowed seven times, in 1939, 1942, 1945, 1952–53, 1980, and 1985, second only to Green Bay (10). [LINK: ([271]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-272]

The Redskins had 61 defensive turnovers in  [LINK: (1983) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1983_Washington_Redskins_season] , the third most all-time. [LINK: ([272]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-273]  The turnover differential of +43 that year was the highest of all time.

The Redskins had only 12 defensive turnovers in  [LINK: (2006) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2006_Washington_Redskins_season] , the fewest in a 16-game season and second all time (the  [LINK: (Baltimore Colts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baltimore_Colts]  had 11 turnovers in the strike-shortened  [LINK: (1982 season) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1982_NFL_season]  which lasted only nine games.) [LINK: ([273]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-274]

Special teams

The Redskins led the league in field goals for eight seasons,  [LINK: (1945) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1945_NFL_season] ,  [LINK: (1956) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1956_NFL_season] ,  [LINK: (1971) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1971_NFL_season] ,  [LINK: (1976) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1976_NFL_season] –77,  [LINK: (1979) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1979_NFL_season] ,  [LINK: (1982) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1982_NFL_season] ,  [LINK: (1992) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1992_NFL_season] . Only the  [LINK: (Green Bay Packers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Bay_Packers]  have ever led more. [LINK: ([274]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-fieldgoals-275]

The Redskins and Bears attempted an NFL record 11 field goals on November 14, 1971, and the Redskins and Giants tied that mark on November 14, 1976. [LINK: ([274]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-fieldgoals-275]

The Redskins 28 consecutive games, from  [LINK: (1988) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1988_NFL_season]  to  [LINK: (1990) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1990_NFL_season] , scoring a field goal is third all time. [LINK: ([274]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-fieldgoals-275]

The Redskins have led the league in punting average six times, in 1940–43, 1945, and 1958, second only to the  [LINK: (Denver Broncos) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denver_Broncos] . [LINK: ([275]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-punting-276]  Their four consecutive years from 1940 to 1943 is an NFL record. [LINK: ([275]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-punting-276]

The Redskins have led the league in average kickoff return yards eight times, in 1942, 1947, 1962–63, 1973–74, 1981, and 1995, more than any other team. [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-277]


# BROADCASTING #

The Commanders'  [LINK: (flagship station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flagship_station]  is  [LINK: (Big 100) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_100]  (WBIG-FM), which acquired the rights in 2022 as part of an agreement with  [LINK: (iHeartMedia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IHeartMedia] . [LINK: ([277]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-278]   [LINK: (Frank Herzog) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Herzog]  served the team's lead  [LINK: (play-by-play) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Play-by-play]  announcer from 1979 until 2004, when he was replaced by Larry Michael. [LINK: ([278]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-279]  Herzog was joined from 1981 by former Redskins  [LINK: (Sonny Jurgensen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonny_Jurgensen]  and  [LINK: (Sam Huff) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sam_Huff] . [LINK: ([279]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Pals-280]  [LINK: ([280]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-281]  [LINK: ([281]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Allen-282]  Michael retired in 2020 and was replaced by  [LINK: (Bram Weinstein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bram_Weinstein] . [LINK: ([282]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-283]  [LINK: ([283]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-284]  Weinstein is joined by color commentators and former players  [LINK: (London Fletcher) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Fletcher]  and  [LINK: (Logan Paulsen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logan_Paulsen] .  [LINK: (Julie Donaldson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julie_Donaldson]  served as host from 2020 to 2023 and was the first woman to be an on-air broadcaster for an NFL team. [LINK: ([284]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-285]  [LINK: ([285]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-286]

[LINK: (WMAL) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WSBN]  served as the team's flagship station from 1942 to 1956 and from 1963 through 1991. [LINK: ([279]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Pals-280]  [LINK: ([281]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Allen-282]  From 1992 to 1994,  [LINK: (WTEM) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WTEM] —which had become DC's first all-sports radio station—became the Redskins' flagship station after which they moved to  [LINK: (WJFK-FM) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WJFK-FM] . [LINK: ([281]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Allen-282]  [LINK: ([286]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-287]  In 2006, after WJFK and  [LINK: (CBS Radio) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS_Radio]  declined to renew its rights to the team, the Redskins moved to  [LINK: (WWXT) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWXT] ,  [LINK: (WWXX) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWXX] , and  [LINK: (WXTR) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WTNT_(AM)] . The stations had recently been bought by  [LINK: (Red Zebra Broadcasting) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Zebra_Broadcasting] —a group co-owned by Snyder—and had become a sports radio  [LINK: (trimulcast) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trimulcast]  known as "Triple X  [LINK: (ESPN Radio) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ESPN_Radio] ". [LINK: ([287]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-288]  Red Zebra Broadcasting would eventually acquire WTEM in 2008, making it the originating station of the simulcast. [LINK: ([288]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-289]  [LINK: ([281]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Allen-282]

In 2017,  [LINK: (Cumulus Media) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulus_Media]  reached an agreement to carry the team's radio broadcasts on WMAL, marking its return to the station for the first time since 1991. As part of the agreement, WTEM remained the team's official flagship. [LINK: ([281]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Allen-282]  In 2018, WTEM was sold to  [LINK: (Urban One) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_One] , but maintained its rights to the team. [LINK: ([289]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-290]  [LINK: ([290]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-291]  In June 2019, WMAL flipped to sports radio itself as  [LINK: (WSBN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WSBN] , taking over the local ESPN Radio affiliation from WTEM; WMAL's previous  [LINK: (conservative talk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_talk]  format moved exclusively to its  [LINK: (FM simulcast station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WMAL-FM] . [LINK: ([291]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-292]  [LINK: ([292]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-293]

Telecasts of preseason games were previously carried by  [LINK: (NBC Sports Washington) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBC_Sports_Washington]  in the  [LINK: (Mid-Atlantic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mid-Atlantic_states]  region. Sister  [LINK: (NBC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBC]  owned-and-operated station  [LINK: (WRC-TV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WRC-TV]  was the team's "official" broadcast television station, simulcasting the preseason games and airing other team-produced programs during the season. [LINK: ([293]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Sports_Business_Journal-2023-294]  [LINK: ([294]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-295]  With the sale of NBC Sports Washington by  [LINK: (NBCUniversal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NBCUniversal]  to  [LINK: (Washington Capitals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Capitals]  and  [LINK: (Washington Wizards) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Wizards]  owner  [LINK: (Ted Leonsis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ted_Leonsis] , the network declined to renew its rights to the team. [LINK: ([293]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-Sports_Business_Journal-2023-294]  Most regular season Commanders games are carried by  [LINK: (NFL on Fox) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NFL_on_Fox] . In June 2024, the team and  [LINK: (WUSA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WUSA_(TV)]  announced a partnership to broadcast the team's preseason games as well as some original content. [LINK: ([295]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-296]


# U.S. PRESIDENTIAL ELECTION SUPERSTITION #

Between 1932 and 2008, for 19 of 20  [LINK: (United States presidential elections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_presidential_election] , a win for the Redskins in their last home game prior to  [LINK: (Election Day) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Election_Day_(United_States)]  coincided with the incumbent party winning re-election. The exception was in 2004, when  [LINK: (Republican) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_Party_(United_States)]  incumbent  [LINK: (George W. Bush) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_W._Bush]  won re-election despite the  [LINK: (Green Bay Packers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Bay_Packers]  beating the Redskins. [LINK: ([296]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-297]  The trend subsequently came to an end when in 2012,  [LINK: (Democratic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_(United_States)]  incumbent  [LINK: (Barack Obama) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barack_Obama]  won re-election despite the Redskins losing to the  [LINK: (Carolina Panthers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carolina_Panthers] . It has since been wrong for the elections in 2016, 2020, and 2024. [LINK: ([297]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-298]  [LINK: ([298]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Commanders#cite_note-i822-299]


